# How Long Does It Take to Build a Website?

If you're planning to build a website for your business, personal brand, or new idea, one of your first questions is probably: *How long will it take?*

You might need a website to promote your services, attract customers, sell products, showcase your work, or establish a professional online presence. Whatever your goal, you probably don't want to wait forever before launching.

The honest answer is that **building a website can take anywhere from a few days to several months**, depending on what you're building and what's required to complete it.

A simple landing page is very different from a large ecommerce store or a custom web application. The amount of content, design work, functionality, testing, and feedback involved also affects the timeline.

In this guide, we'll look at realistic website development timelines, what happens during the process, and what you can do to get your website launched sooner.

## How Long Does It Take to Build Different Types of Websites?

The time required depends largely on the size and complexity of the project.

Here are general planning estimates for common website types.

{% table %}
- Type of Website
- Estimated Timeline
---
- Simple one-page website
- 2 days to 2 weeks
---
- Landing page for a product or service
- 3 days to 2 weeks
---
- Personal portfolio website
- 1 to 4 weeks
---
- Basic business website
- 2 to 6 weeks
---
- Professional custom business website
- 4 to 10 weeks
---
- Blog or content-focused website
- 2 to 6 weeks
---
- Ecommerce website
- 4 to 12 weeks
---
- Website with advanced integrations
- 6 to 16 weeks or more
---
- Custom web application
- 2 to 6 months or more
{% /table %}

These are indicative estimates, not guaranteed delivery dates. They assume a reasonably defined project and timely access to the information, content, and approvals needed to move forward.

A complex project may take longer, while a well-prepared, straightforward website may launch sooner.

Let's look at what each timeline means.

## 1. Simple One-Page Website: 2 Days to 2 Weeks

A one-page website presents essential information on a single page. Visitors can learn about you or your business, explore your offer, and find a way to contact you.

It might include:

- A short introduction
- Your services or offer
- A few images or examples of your work
- Testimonials or other trust signals
- Contact information
- A call to action

This type of website is useful for freelancers, consultants, small businesses, and individuals who need a straightforward online presence.

The timeline can be short when the design is simple, the content is ready, and no complex features are required.

However, a one-page website can take longer if you need custom illustrations, several design revisions, copywriting, or additional functionality.

## 2. Landing Page: 3 Days to 2 Weeks

A landing page is designed around a specific goal.

That goal might be getting visitors to book a consultation, sign up for a service, download a resource, or purchase a product.

Unlike a general business website, a landing page focuses on guiding visitors toward one main action.

The development process may involve:

- Understanding the target audience
- Planning the page structure
- Writing persuasive, relevant content
- Designing the page
- Adding forms or other conversion features
- Testing the experience on mobile and desktop

A simple landing page can be built quickly. A more detailed page that requires custom graphics, integrations, analytics, or extensive content may need additional time.

The important thing is not simply to finish the page quickly. It should also communicate the offer clearly and make it easy for visitors to take the next step.

## 3. Personal Portfolio Website: 1 to 4 Weeks

A portfolio website helps people see your skills, experience, projects, and professional identity.

Photographers, designers, developers, writers, musicians, consultants, and other professionals can use portfolios to showcase their work and attract opportunities.

A typical portfolio might include:

- A homepage or introduction
- An About section
- A portfolio or project gallery
- Services or areas of expertise
- Testimonials or client results
- Contact details

The main factor affecting the timeline is often the quality and availability of the portfolio content.

If your projects are already organized and your images and descriptions are ready, development can move faster.

If you still need to select projects, prepare images, write case studies, or refine your personal brand, those tasks can add time.

## 4. Basic Business Website: 2 to 6 Weeks

A basic business website usually contains several pages that explain what a company does and how customers can get in touch.

It might include:

- Home
- About
- Services or Products
- Contact
- Frequently Asked Questions

Some businesses may also need a gallery, testimonials, a booking form, or a small blog.

The timeline depends on the number of pages, the design requirements, and whether the website uses an existing platform or a more customized approach.

A business website also needs more than attractive visuals. Its content should explain what the business offers, who it serves, why customers should trust it, and what visitors should do next.

Planning these details properly can make the development process smoother.

## 5. Professional Custom Business Website: 4 to 10 Weeks

A professional custom website may involve a more distinctive visual identity, carefully planned page layouts, custom interactions, and features tailored to the business.

The process may include:

- Project discovery and planning
- Website structure and user experience design
- Visual design and branding
- Front-end development
- Content integration
- Search engine optimization setup
- Mobile responsiveness
- Performance and functionality testing
- Launch preparation

A relatively straightforward custom business website may be completed sooner than this range. Projects with more pages, extensive content, complex integrations, or multiple rounds of revisions may take longer.

Custom development does not automatically mean a better website. The value comes from choosing an approach that fits your goals, audience, budget, and technical requirements.

If you're considering a custom website, read our guide to [custom websites vs website builders](/articles/custom-website-vs-website-builder-which-is-better-for-your-business).

## 6. Ecommerce Website: 4 to 12 Weeks

An ecommerce website allows customers to browse products, place orders, and complete purchases online.

Depending on the business, it may require:

- Product categories and product pages
- Product images and descriptions
- Shopping cart and checkout
- Payment gateway integration
- Shipping or delivery options
- Order management
- Inventory management
- Customer notifications
- Security checks and transaction testing

A small store with a limited number of products and straightforward requirements can launch relatively quickly.

A larger store with hundreds of products, complex delivery rules, discounts, customer accounts, or third-party integrations will generally take longer.

Payment integration and checkout testing deserve particular attention. A store is not ready simply because its product pages look good. Customers must also be able to complete orders reliably.

## 7. Custom Web Application: 2 to 6 Months or More

A web application is more complex than a typical informational website because it allows users to perform tasks and interact with data.

Examples include:

- Booking and scheduling platforms
- Customer dashboards
- Project management tools
- Online calculators with advanced functionality
- Membership platforms
- Business management systems
- SaaS products

Depending on the project, development may involve user accounts, databases, permissions, APIs, payment systems, dashboards, and automated processes.

A small application with clearly defined requirements may be completed in a few months. A larger product with many features, multiple user types, or strict security and reliability requirements can take considerably longer.

For these projects, it's often better to build and launch a useful first version before expanding it with additional features.

That way, you can validate the idea without waiting until every possible feature has been developed.

## What Happens During the Website Development Process?

To understand the timeline, it helps to know what actually happens between the initial idea and the finished website.

Here are the main stages.

### Stage 1: Planning and Discovery

**Typical duration: 1 to 5 working days**

Before design begins, the project needs a clear direction.

This stage establishes the website's purpose, target audience, essential pages, required features, and overall scope.

For example, a photographer may need a portfolio, service information, and an enquiry form. A consulting firm may need service pages, case studies, and a booking system.

The clearer these requirements are, the easier it becomes to estimate the work.

Changing the project's direction after development has started can create additional work and delay the launch.

### Stage 2: Website Structure and Design

**Typical duration: 3 to 10 working days**

This stage determines how the website will look and how visitors will navigate it.

It may include page layouts, typography, color choices, image selection, navigation, and mobile layouts.

Some projects need detailed wireframes and design prototypes. Others can move forward with a simpler design process.

The amount of design work depends on the project's complexity and the number of pages that require unique layouts.

Timely feedback also matters. When a design is submitted for review, having one clear set of comments can help avoid unnecessary revision cycles.

### Stage 3: Development

**Typical duration: 5 to 20 working days for many small and medium websites**

During development, the approved design and website structure are turned into a working website.

This may involve building page layouts, integrating content, configuring the content management system, connecting forms, and implementing the required functionality.

Some features can be developed quickly. Others require more careful implementation and testing.

The duration also depends on the development approach. A website built using a suitable existing platform may take less time than a fully customized solution with complex requirements.

### Stage 4: Content Integration and SEO Setup

**Typical duration: 2 to 7 working days**

A website needs useful content before it can serve its purpose.

This stage involves adding text, images, service descriptions, product details, and other relevant information.

Basic SEO preparation may include:

- Writing descriptive page titles and meta descriptions
- Organizing headings logically
- Creating clear, descriptive URLs
- Adding appropriate image alt text
- Setting up internal links
- Checking indexability and relevant technical settings
- Adding structured data where appropriate

Some of this work can happen alongside design and development rather than after them.

If content is ready early, it can be integrated as the website is built. If the content is incomplete, the project may need to pause while the missing material is prepared.

For a deeper look at improving visibility in AI-powered search, read our guide to [Generative Engine Optimization (GEO)](/articles/generative-engine-optimization-geo-how-to-optimize-your-website-for-ai-search).

### Stage 5: Testing and Revisions

**Typical duration: 2 to 7 working days**

Before launch, the website needs to be checked carefully.

Testing may cover:

- Mobile and desktop layouts
- Navigation and links
- Contact forms
- Buttons and calls to action
- Images and page content
- Loading performance
- Browser compatibility
- HTTPS and basic security configuration
- Forms, payments, or other important integrations

This is also when agreed corrections are made.

The amount of time required depends on the size of the website, the features involved, and the number of issues discovered.

Skipping testing to launch sooner can create problems for visitors and cost more time later.

### Stage 6: Launch

**Typical duration: 1 to 3 working days**

Launch involves making the website publicly available and checking that everything works in its live environment.

Depending on the project, this can include:

- Connecting the domain
- Configuring hosting
- Enabling HTTPS
- Checking important pages and forms
- Verifying mobile functionality
- Setting up analytics or search monitoring
- Submitting the sitemap to Google Search Console when appropriate

A straightforward launch may take only a few hours. DNS changes, hosting problems, or third-party configuration issues can extend the process.

Also, launching the website does not guarantee that Google will index it immediately. Search engines need time to discover and process pages, and indexing is not guaranteed.

## What Can Delay a Website Project?

Even a well-planned website can take longer than expected. Here are some of the most common reasons.

### 1. Missing Content

If the developer is waiting for your business description, service details, product images, or contact information, progress may slow down.

**How to avoid it:** Prepare the essential content before development begins. If you need help with copywriting or photography, agree on that as part of the project.

### 2. Unclear Requirements

A project that begins without a clear scope can grow as new ideas appear.

You might start with five pages, then request a blog, booking system, customer dashboard, and several integrations.

Some additions may be worthwhile, but they can change the timeline and cost.

**How to avoid it:** Agree on the essential pages, features, deliverables, and launch goals before work starts. Treat additional features as changes that may require a revised estimate.

### 3. Slow Feedback and Approvals

A developer may complete a design or feature quickly, but the project can still stall if feedback takes several days or weeks.

**How to avoid it:** Agree on review deadlines and appoint one person to provide consolidated feedback.

### 4. Too Many Revisions

Repeatedly changing the design, content, colors, layout, or direction can extend the project.

**How to avoid it:** Establish your priorities early and review the design carefully before development progresses too far. Agree on how many revision rounds are included in the project.

### 5. Complex Features

Booking systems, payment gateways, customer accounts, external APIs, and custom dashboards require additional implementation and testing.

**How to avoid it:** Separate essential launch features from improvements that can be introduced later.

### 6. Domain, Hosting, or Third-Party Issues

Problems with domain access, hosting configuration, payment provider approval, or external services can delay launch.

**How to avoid it:** Confirm ownership and access to the required accounts early, and identify any third-party approvals the project depends on.

### 7. Poor Planning

Starting development without agreeing on the scope, responsibilities, content, or timeline can lead to confusion.

**How to avoid it:** Ask for a clear project plan that identifies the major milestones, what you need to provide, and what counts as completion.

## How Can You Get Your Website Built Faster?

If you want to launch sooner, there are several practical things you can do.

**1. Know what you want the website to achieve.** Decide whether your main goal is to attract enquiries, showcase your work, sell products, or provide a service.

**2. Start with the essential pages.** You may not need every feature at launch. Build what is necessary to serve your audience, then improve the website over time.

**3. Prepare your content early.** Gather your logo, brand colors, images, business information, service descriptions, and contact details.

**4. Choose the right development approach.** A website builder may be suitable for a simple project with a limited budget. Custom development may make more sense when you need a specific design or functionality.

**5. Give feedback promptly.** Clear, consolidated feedback helps keep the project moving.

**6. Keep the scope realistic.** Avoid adding major features halfway through development unless they are genuinely necessary.

**7. Work with someone who communicates clearly.** You should understand what is happening, what comes next, and whether anything is putting the delivery date at risk.

A fast project is not necessarily a rushed project. Good preparation and clear communication often make a bigger difference than trying to skip important steps.

## Does a More Expensive Website Take Longer to Build?

Not necessarily.

Price and timeline are related to some extent, but they measure different things.

A website can be expensive because it requires specialist expertise, a highly customized design, advanced integrations, or ongoing support. A relatively affordable website may still take time if the content is complicated or the project requires many revisions.

Likewise, a higher budget does not automatically mean a shorter delivery time. Adding more people to a project can help with certain tasks, but coordination, approvals, and dependencies still matter.

When comparing proposals, look beyond the promised delivery date. Check what the price includes, what you need to provide, how revisions are handled, and what happens after launch.

If you're budgeting for a website in Nigeria, our guide to [how much a website costs in Nigeria](/articles/how-much-does-a-website-cost-in-nigeria) explains the main cost factors and typical price ranges.

## Can You Launch a Website in One Day?

Yes, in some circumstances.

A simple page using a prepared template, ready-made content, and minimal customization may be published within a day.

However, that doesn't mean every website can or should be built in a day.

A professional business website may require planning, content preparation, design decisions, technical configuration, testing, and revisions. More complex websites need even more work.

Be careful when a provider promises to build a complete website overnight without explaining what is included.

Ask whether the offer covers mobile responsiveness, your actual content, contact forms, domain configuration, security, testing, and any required integrations.

The goal is not simply to get something online. It is to launch a website that works for your business and your visitors.

## How Long Does It Take for a Website to Appear on Google?

Building a website and getting it indexed by Google are two different processes.

A developer may finish and publish your website on schedule, but Google may take additional time to discover and index its pages. There is no guaranteed indexing deadline.

The process can depend on whether Google can access the pages, the site's technical setup, internal links, sitemap availability, and other factors.

After launch, you can use Google Search Console to inspect important URLs, submit your sitemap, and monitor indexing reports.

Indexing also does not guarantee that your website will rank highly for your target keywords. Search visibility develops over time and depends on relevance, competition, content quality, technical factors, and other signals.

If appearing in search results is important to your business, consider SEO during the planning and development stages rather than treating it as an afterthought.

## What Should You Ask a Website Developer About the Timeline?

Before hiring someone to build your website, ask these questions:

- How long will the project take from kickoff to launch?
- What pages and features are included?
- What do you need from me before development begins?
- When will I review the design?
- How many rounds of revisions are included?
- What could delay the project?
- Will I be able to review the website before launch?
- Does the timeline include testing and domain configuration?
- What happens if I request additional features?
- What support is available after launch?

These questions help establish realistic expectations and reduce misunderstandings.

A trustworthy developer should be able to explain the proposed timeline and the assumptions behind it. Exact dates may depend on information that is not yet available, but the process should still be clear.

## Final Thoughts

So, how long does it take to build a website?

A simple landing page might take a few days, a standard business website may need several weeks, and a complex ecommerce platform or web application can take months.

The right timeline depends on your goals, the features you need, the amount of content involved, and how efficiently the project moves through planning, design, development, testing, and launch.

You don't always need a large website to make a strong first impression. Start with what your audience needs, focus on doing those things well, and expand when your business is ready.

